\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The cellulation $P^0\sqcup P^1\sqcup P^2$ of a flat pentagon, drawn here as a concrete 2-dimensional warm-up before the general definitions below: five $0$-cells $e^0_j$, five $1$-cells $e^1_{ij}$ suspended between nearest-neighbor vertices, and a single $2$-cell $e^2_\Delta $ (shaded) whose boundary $\Delta $ is the whole perimeter. This is the flat, cyclic/dihedral case mentioned above, with $\mathrm  {rot}\hspace  {1mm}P=D_5$; the same $j\DOTSB \mapstochar \rightarrow j+1$ pattern of vertices and edges recurs, unchanged in spirit, for every 3-dimensional polyhedron in the rest of the paper.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
