\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The derived polyhedron $DP^1$ (red) of a regular $n$-gon $P$ (blue), obtained by placing a new vertex at the centroid of each $1$-cell. Elementary geometry gives $DP^1$ as an $n$-gon rotated by $\pi /n$ and scaled by $\cos (\pi /n)$ relative to $P$, for any $n$. For $n=3$ this $60^\circ $ tilt happens to coincide with a $180^\circ $ point-reflection, since $180^\circ \equiv 60^\circ \pmod  {120^\circ }$ for a triangle's $3$-fold symmetry, producing the familiar hexagram (``Star of David''); no such coincidence occurs for $n=4$ or larger $n$. We will meet the same picture one dimension up in Section\nonbreakingspace \ref {sec:altsym}, where a cube's eight vertices split into two oppositely-oriented inscribed tetrahedra (the \emph  {stella octangula}).}}{9}{}\protected@file@percent }
